{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And he sets them before YHWH.</b> These two he-goats were a gift to the Holy One, blessed be He, both before the lot and after the lot. Before the lot — this is what is meant by 'and he sets them before YHWH,' to make clear that both of them were given as a gift to the Holy One, blessed be He, and set before Him. And after the lot — this is what is meant by 'and the he-goat upon which the lot for Azazel came up shall stand alive before YHWH,' to say that even that one for Azazel was given to the Holy One, blessed be He. And it is explained in the parashah: the he-goat for YHWH was slaughtered, as it is written 'and prepare it as a sin-offering,' but the he-goat for Azazel was not slaughtered; rather, it was sent away to the wilderness, in the manner of the birds of the leper (Leviticus 14:7) 'upon the face of the field,' and in the manner of the heifer whose neck is broken (Deuteronomy 21:4) in a wadi, an earthen one, that is neither worked nor sown. And the intention of the Torah is not to sacrifice to Azazel — far be it! For there is no worshipper who intends in his prayer and service anything but the Holy One, blessed be He, alone, as it is written (Exodus 22:19): ״זובח לאלהים יחרם בלתי לה' לבדו״. And if one were to intend toward intermediaries, his service would not be accepted and his sacrifice would not find favor, but rather 'the sacrifice of the wicked is an abomination' (Proverbs 21:27). And this is the principle of all the offerings, every one — that we are obligated to direct our service to YHWH alone, for He is the One and the First. And if perhaps other beings receive, through our service, some mode of reception, we do not concern ourselves with this at all, for thus we have been commanded: that our intention be fixed upon the unique Name, exalted be He, alone. And because of this, these two he-goats were both a gift to the Holy One, blessed be He, to make clear that our intention in both of them is only toward the unique Name alone. And therefore the priest would do these two things with them at the beginning of his rite: he would give them both as a gift to the Holy One, blessed be He, and he would cast lots upon them — because a thing divided by lot is the portion that comes from YHWH, as it is written (Proverbs 16:33): ״בחיק יוטל את הגורל ומה' כל משפטו״. For had the priest consecrated them verbally and said of them, 'this one is for YHWH and this one is for Azazel,' he would have equated matter with matter and the matter would have been like a kind of service to Azazel — and it is not possible to do so. But now that it was by lot, behold, the Holy One, blessed be He, gives that he-goat to Azazel, and from Him it comes to it, and He chooses — not we. And from this Onkelos translated: 'one lot for YHWH — לשמא דה'׳ — for that he-goat which is for YHWH is for His Name and not for Azazel; and the second is for Azazel, and not for the name of Azazel.' Thus explained the Ramban, of blessed memory. And the Sages, of blessed memory, stated explicitly in tractate Yoma, at the beginning of the chapter 'Trop ba-Kalpi' (Yoma 39a), that if he did not cast the lot he has done nothing, and there they concluded that the casting of the lot is indispensable." "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat),</b> the explanation of the word עזאזל — it is difficult: from the language of עזוז (might), for it was a high mountain, a hard cliff, in the manner of what is written 'ארץ גזרה' (a land cut off). And so we find in Torat Kohanim: 'לעזאזל — the hardest place among the mountains. One might think in inhabited territory — Scripture says: המדברה. And from where do we know it must be a cliff? Scripture says: אל ארץ גזרה.' And so explained Rashi, of blessed memory." "<b>And by way of the Midrash,</b> they said in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer: Therefore they would give him, to Samael, a bribe on Yom HaKippurim, שלא יקרב את קרבנם, as it is said: 'one lot for YHWH and one lot for Azazel.' The lot of the Holy One, blessed be He, is for the elevation-offering, and the lot of Azazel is the sin-offering he-goat, and all the iniquities of Israel are upon it, as it is said: 'ונשא השעיר עליו את כל עונותם.' Samael saw that no sin was found in them on Yom HaKippurim, and said before the Holy One, blessed be He: 'Master of the worlds! You have one nation on earth like the ministering angels in heaven. Just as the ministering angels are barefoot, so Israel are barefoot on Yom HaKippurim. Just as the ministering angels have no eating and drinking, so Israel have no eating and drinking on Yom HaKippurim. Just as the ministering angels have no kneeling, so Israel stand on their feet on Yom HaKippurim. Just as the ministering angels have peace among them, so Israel have peace among them on Yom HaKippurim. Just as the ministering angels are clean of all sin, so Israel are clean of all sin on Yom HaKippurim.' And the Holy One, blessed be He, hears the testimony of Israel from their accuser, and makes atonement for the sanctuary, and for the altar, and for the priests, and for all the congregation of the people, as it is said: 'וכפר את מקדש הקדש ואת אהל מועד ואת המזבח יכפר ועל הכהנים ועל כל עם הקהל יכפר.' Thus far in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 46. And the phrase שלא יקרב את קרבנם — there are versions that write שלו with a vav, and there are those that write שלא with an alef. And whether it is written שלו with a vav or with an alef, I do not know its meaning, for the language does not settle properly according to the matter, neither this way nor that. And it is possible that the version of the phrase is שלא יקרב את קרבנם with an alef, and that the word יקרב is from the language of קרב and warfare, as if to say: that he not attack their offering — for he is the evil advocate, and he should speak well of Israel." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> the master revealed from his mysteries through this attribute, that which is clear in it: the word עזאזל means 'a mighty force,' and the word is as though written עזיאל, from the language of עז and אילות, for thus the supernal forces are sealed with the name אל. And this is what is written (Psalms 35:10): ״מציל עני מחזק ממנו״, and the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted it of the good inclination and the evil inclination. And just as it is called חזק (strong), so it is called מלך (king), for thus Solomon, peace be upon him, called it (Ecclesiastes 4:13): ״מלך זקן וכסיל״. And you already know the saying of the Sages, of blessed memory: 'He is the adversary (Satan), he is the evil inclination, he is the angel of death.'" "<b>And if</b> you are roused to the number of the letters of מלך (Melekh), you will find it equal to the number of the letters of מאדים (Mars). And this is the proof, for it rules over every living creature under the sun, and it is the soul of that sphere, and it is known by its name, for the seal of its name is אל, like the names of the rest of the angels. And in its name there is an allusion, for it is סם המות (the poison of death) to all who do not guard against it; and further, that it stands at a person's left; and further, that it includes the language of סמיות (blindness), because it accepts bribes, as it is written (Deuteronomy 16:19): ״כי השחד יעור״. And the masters of truth expanded the explanation of this from what they said in their midrash: 'ונשא השעיר עליו את כל עונותם' — the iniquities of a perfect man (איש תם), as it is said (Genesis 25:27): ״ויעקב איש תם״." "<b>And the Midrash</b> of Pirkei R. Eliezer illumines all of this completely, for there it mentions him by name, and behold, it is a strong force, explicitly set forth from within the word עזאזל, as I have mentioned. And moreover the word עזאזל embraces the expression עז אזל — 'the goat departed.' And you will find from the first occurrence of the word עזאזל until the word לשעירים there are 33 verses after that word, and from this the sage R. Abraham, of blessed memory, wrote of the matter in allusion, and this is his language: 'If you are able to understand the secret of the word that comes after the word עזאזל, you will know his secret and the secret of his name, for he has companions in Scripture. And I will reveal to you a little of the secret by allusion: when you are thirty-three years old you will know it.' Thus far his language." "<b>And from this</b> it will be revealed to us for all of this: to whom the sent-goat is sent, and what is his name and what is his land and all the places of his dominion. For they are places of desolation and ruin, for he is the prince of the wilderness; and all the wars and the tumults and every stroke of sword and killing and destruction are woven from the emanation of his force, and all who draw the sword among the nations draw strength from him. And he is the one who receives this goat, for the goats and the he-goats among the animals are in his portion, and among the nations, the seed of Esau, who is called שעיר, as it is said (Genesis 27:11): ״הן עשו אחי איש שעיר״; and his nation too is called שעיר, as it is said (Numbers 24:18): ״והיה ירשה שעיר אויביו״. And likewise in his portion are the demons — the slanderers and the harmers — who are called שעירים, as it is said (Isaiah 13:21): ״ושעירים ירקדו שם״. And they said in the Midrash that the Holy One, blessed be He, cast him and his retinue down from the heavens, and he seized the wings of Michael to pull him down with him, but the Holy One, blessed be He, delivered him; and therefore Michael is called פליט. If so, he is below the sefirot, and he is a dividing barrier between the sefirot and the offerings. And how could his offering ascend to the unified Name when he stands in the middle, intervening on the way, standing to slander, holding back his offering? Therefore it said that he should not prosecute — that is, that he should not interpose between the unified Name and his offering. For when this slaughter is given to him, like one who tosses a morsel to a dog so that it not bite him, then his offering will ascend by a paved road to the unified Name without prosecution, since we have prepared for him and for his forces and for all the children of Israel — no dog will sharpen its tongue, and the prosecutor turns defender. Even though he derives benefit along with his forces, our intention is solely toward the Master of all, who commanded us thus to give a gift to one of His ministers and servants for our benefit, so that he may speak well on our behalf. And this gift does not come to him from us but from Him, blessed be He — to the point that even at the time of the lottery, when sometimes the lot of עזאזל falls on the right and that of HaShem on the left, they were not to swap them. And so they said in the Gemara of tractate Yoma: they asked R. Akiva, what about swapping the left to the right and the right to the left, so that the lot of HaShem returns to the right? And he answered them: Do not give an opening to the sectarians to rule. That is to say: if they were to swap them, the sectarians would say — עזאזל is an independent authority unto itself, but there is another, greater authority; therefore he needed to honor it and restore it to the right. And they do not know that these are decrees of the Holy One, blessed be He, and He Himself gives and concedes His honor for our benefit. And if this one derives benefit from what we give, we are not giving him anything, nor do we intend toward him at all, but only toward the unified Name. And so we find with the angel of Manoah, who derived benefit when he ascended in the flame of the altar, and Manoah's intention was not toward the angel, heaven forbid, but toward the unified Name. And likewise the portions in the offerings that the Torah assigned to Aaron and his sons — even though they derive benefit from this, there is in the matter no aspect whatever of worship directed to the priests who offer the offerings, but only to do the will of our Father in heaven, who commanded us thus."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:07.236564927Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}